\begin{abstract}
Training-free LLM information extraction degrades as schema complexity grows, yet prior work conflates two factors: type count and type confusability. We present what is, to our knowledge, the first controlled study isolating these factors across NER, RE, and event detection on four datasets. We quantify confusability via the Schema Confusability Score (SCS) and its batch-level aggregation, the Batch Confusability Score (BCS). Degradation operates along two dimensions with task-dependent effect structures: count-related recall decline (up to $-$49\,pp on DocRED 96-relation RE) and confusability-related type substitution. In RE, BCS explains 22--58\% of cross-batch accuracy variance (FewRel/DocRED), independent of output format (paired constrained/unconstrained comparison yields virtually identical correlations); NER shows no batch-level BCS signal, as errors scatter across missed entities and boundary failures. Schema decomposition fails due to merge amplification (NER, ED) and routing failure (RE). Both dimensions replicate across four models spanning three architecture families (Qwen, LLaMA, Gemini; 8B--70B), two decoding formats, and five prompt designs.
\end{abstract}

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}

We evaluate how prompt schema size and inter-type confusability affect zero-shot LLM extraction across three information extraction tasks and four datasets: named entity recognition (NER), sentence-level relation extraction (RE), document-level RE, and event detection (ED).

\subsection{Tasks and Datasets}
\label{sec:datasets}

\paragraph{Few-NERD (NER).}
Few-NERD \citep{fewnerd-2021} provides 66 fine-grained entity types organized into 8 coarse categories (person, location, organization, building, art, product, event, other). The dataset spans diverse domains and is suitable for cross-domain evaluation. We sample 1{,}000 instances uniformly from the test set; type frequency follows the natural long-tail distribution of Few-NERD. Evaluation uses token-level text matching. Pairwise analysis covers 1{,}953 type pairs with non-zero co-occurrence out of $\binom{66}{2}=2{,}145$ possible pairs.

\paragraph{FewRel (RE).}
FewRel \citep{fewrel-2018} contains 80 relation types sourced from Wikidata. Since the validation split covers only 16 relations, we combine the training and validation splits to obtain the full 80-relation pool required for schema-scale experiments, and sample 1{,}000 balanced instances (12--13 per relation type, stratified to ensure equal representation). A held-out validation confirms SCS--confusion generalization across the split boundary (Appendix~\ref{app:scs-heldout-validation}). Each instance provides a sentence with marked head and tail entities; the task is to classify the relation. FewRel's balanced design (700 instances per relation type) eliminates type frequency as a potential confound; this balance makes it the primary dataset for BCS analysis.

\paragraph{DocRED (Document-level RE).}
DocRED \citep{docred-2019} contains 96 relation types sourced from Wikidata, evaluated at the document level where entity pairs may span multiple sentences. We sample 1{,}000 instances balanced across 96 relation types ($\sim$10 per type) from the DocRED development set and formulate the task identically to FewRel: given a document with marked head and tail entities, classify the relation. DocRED extends the RE evaluation to a larger schema (96 vs.\ 80 types) and document-level context, testing whether confusability effects persist under longer input contexts and cross-sentence reasoning.

