\label{sec:conclusion}
Training-free LLM extraction degrades along two separable dimensions---count-associated recall decline and confusability-associated type substitution---replicated on four models from three architecture families (8B--70B open-weight; Gemini 2.5 Flash via API) for RE; NER null confirmed on two models; decomposition fails via merge amplification and routing failure.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Unless otherwise noted, all experiments in this section use the v1 prompt (\S\ref{sec:model}; full templates in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompts}).

\subsection{Count-Degradation Curves}
\label{sec:count_curves}

Figure~\ref{fig:main_results}a shows RE accuracy declines monotonically with schema size (0.86$\to$0.50 for FewRel). NER recall declines through intermediate counts (0.51$\to$0.27, plateauing across counts 22--44) before partial recovery at full-schema (0.41), where the elimination of type-filtering overhead restores extraction performance. DocRED replicates the monotonic RE pattern (88.0\%$\to$38.7\%, $-$56\%; Appendix). MAVEN exhibits a reversed trend attributable to centrality confounding (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}). The effect replicates on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct ($-$43\,pp on Few-NERD; per-batch $r = 0.725$; Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}). The pattern is stable across three seeds (max $\Delta = 10.7$\,pp, within 1\,SD; intra-batch variance exceeds inter-seed variance by ${>}$5$\times$; Appendix~\ref{app:variance-decomposition}). Formal trend analysis confirms monotonic RE decline (Jonckheere-Terpstra $J = 109{,}707$, $p < 0.001$). Count curves establish trend direction; magnitude estimation relies on the higher-powered BCS-controlled experiments (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}).

\subsection{Pairwise Confusability and Type Confusion}
\label{sec:pairwise}

We test whether pairwise SCS predicts type-level confusion rates. For each type pair $(t_i, t_j)$, we compute the confusion rate (the fraction of $t_i$ gold instances misclassified as $t_j$) and correlate it with $\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j)$. Table~\ref{tab:pairwise} reports Pearson correlations with bootstrap 95\% CIs.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Dataset} & \textbf{Embed.} & \textbf{$n$ pairs} & \textbf{$r$} & \textbf{$r^2$} & \textbf{95\% CI} & \textbf{$p$} \\
\midrule
Few-NERD & Qwen3 & 1{,}953 & 0.112 & 0.013 & {[}0.076, 0.156{]} & ${<}\,0.001$ \\
MAVEN & MiniLM & 11{,}026 & 0.129 & 0.017 & {[}0.104, 0.156{]} & ${<}\,0.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Pairwise SCS--confusion correlation. Confusion rate is bidirectional $C(t_i{\to}t_j) + C(t_j{\to}t_i)$ divided by gold frequency sum; CIs are percentile bootstrap ($B{=}10{,}000$). FewRel ($r = 0.187$; \S\ref{sec:scs-validation}) excluded (per-instance RE format). Type-level cluster bootstrap CIs exclude zero (Few-NERD [0.069, 0.219]; MAVEN [0.081, 0.185]).}
\label{tab:pairwise}
\end{table}

The correlations are statistically significant but modest: $r^2 \approx 1.3\text{--}1.7\%$. Type-level cluster bootstrap yields wider but still significant CIs (Few-NERD: [0.069, 0.219]; MAVEN: [0.081, 0.185]; $B = 10{,}000$). Spearman correlations corroborate: $\rho = 0.144\text{--}0.227$ across all pairs, strengthening to $\rho = 0.27\text{--}0.31$ among pairs with nonzero confusion (though conditioning on the dependent variable may inflate this).

The remaining variance reflects entity frequency, prompt position \citep{lost-in-middle-2024}, mention ambiguity, and definition quality. While pairwise $r^2$ is modest, correlated type pairs within batches compound this signal: batch-level BCS explains 22--58\% of accuracy variance (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}). The independent MiniLM embedding yields a \emph{stronger} SCS--confusion correlation on Few-NERD ($r = 0.172$ vs.\ Qwen3 $r = 0.138$; Appendix), which indicates that shared-representation bias is conservative. SCS outperforms simpler baselines (Levenshtein, WordNet Wu-Palmer, same-parent; Appendix~\ref{app:scs-baselines}). Appendix~\ref{app:error-analysis} lists the most confusable type pairs with concrete examples.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_error_taxonomy.pdf}
\caption{Error taxonomy decomposition (Few-NERD NER and FewRel RE). IntraSub scales monotonically with BCS across all evaluated datasets ($\rho = 0.84\text{--}1.0$; Table~\ref{tab:error_taxonomy} includes DocRED). RE missed rates remain flat (10--16\%), but NER missed rates are substantially higher (43--65\%) and non-monotonic.}
\label{fig:error_taxonomy}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{BCS-Controlled Experiments}
\label{sec:bcs_controlled}

BCS explains 22--58\% of cross-batch RE accuracy variance; confusability is a substantial predictor alongside count. We fix type count and vary BCS to isolate this effect. FewRel's balanced design (700 instances per type) eliminates type frequency as a confound; the within-pool analysis (\S\ref{sec:within_pool}) controls for pool composition.

In BCS-controlled FewRel subsets, the high-BCS quartile shows $\Delta=-5.3$\,pp lower accuracy than low-BCS (quartile comparison on 100 random batches; based on a single extreme batch per BCS level at 16-rel scale; full data in Appendix~\ref{app:bcs-controlled-table}). At 80 relations, extreme-batch construction amplifies this to $-$31.2\,pp (95\% CI [17.7, 42.7]), stable across count levels. An intermediate count${}\,=\,40$ evaluation (40-relation subsets sampled from the 80-relation pool) yields consistent gaps ($-$33.3\,pp at count 8, $-$31.8\,pp at count 16) and confirms monotonic decline.

\paragraph{Document-level RE (DocRED).}
The effect replicates on DocRED's 96-relation schema ($n{=}55$ per count level): extreme-BCS gap $-$25.0\,pp at count 16 ($\rho = -0.762$) and $-$15.7\,pp at count 32 ($\rho = -0.693$; both $p < 0.001$). Pooled across counts, BCS explains 58\% of cross-batch variance ($R^2 = 0.578$, $n = 110$; 95\% CI [0.46, 0.68]; per-count: $R^2 = 0.653$ at count 16, $0.614$ at count 32), substantially higher than FewRel ($R^2 = 0.218$).

\paragraph{Within-pool controlled comparison.}
\label{sec:within_pool}
Within-pool analysis confirms slope consistency across pools (permutation $p \approx 0.60$; Appendix~\ref{app:within-pool-method}). The 80-relation extreme-batch gap (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}) reflects wider BCS range, not stronger per-unit sensitivity.

\paragraph{Practical magnitude.}
\label{sec:practical_magnitude}
Direct regression on 100 random 16-type batches from the 80-relation pool shows BCS explains 22\% of cross-batch accuracy variance ($R^2 = 0.218$, $p < 0.001$), scaling to 58\% on DocRED (pooled, $n = 110$). The typical BCS span (IQR = 0.054) yields an expected accuracy gap of 2.2\,pp (95\% CI [0.4, 4.0]). Quartile comparison confirms: bottom-quartile BCS batches average 82.8\% vs.\ 78.9\% for top-quartile ($\Delta = 3.9$\,pp). A post-hoc Monte Carlo simulation confirms the ${\sim}3\times$ pair-to-batch amplification (Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation-sim}).

\paragraph{Cross-model validation.}
The effect replicates on Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct: extreme-BCS gaps of $-$27.1\,pp (count 8) and $-$23.9\,pp (count 16), reaching 77--87\% of Qwen3-32B's gap. Both models converge to $\approx$51\% under high confusability, suggesting a task-inherent difficulty floor (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model_bcs}). Gemini 2.5 Flash replicates Dimension~2 at batch level: low-BCS 90.5\% vs.\ high-BCS 72.9\% ($\Delta = -17.6$\,pp, 95\% CI [15.1, 20.1], $n = 50$) at count 8, with the smaller gap likely reflecting ceiling compression from Gemini's higher baseline. Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct (FP8) confirms directional consistency (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_cross_model_70b}, \ref{sec:appendix_cross_model_gemini}). LLaMA-3.1-8B replicates the BCS effect on FewRel RE ($\Delta{=}{-}27.1$\,pp) but shows no batch-level signal on NER, consistent with the task asymmetry observed across all model sizes.

\paragraph{Prompt sensitivity.}
Under the v2 prompt, the extreme-batch BCS gap is $-$25.5\,pp ($r = -0.919$, $p < 0.001$; $n{=}25$), modestly attenuated from v1 but highly significant. NER shows no batch-level BCS--F1 correlation under either prompt ($r \approx 0$, $n = 450$; \S\ref{sec:multi_channel}). The effect holds across four structurally distinct prompt variants at count 32 (all $r \leq -0.47$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompt_variants}), ruling out prompt design as the source. Few-shot demonstrations widen the confusability gap (+4.3\,pp under 5-shot), though this finding is limited to a single dataset and count level (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_fewshot_bcs}).

\subsection{Multi-Dimensional Degradation}
\label{sec:multi_channel}

Error taxonomy decomposition (Table~\ref{tab:error_taxonomy} and Figure~\ref{fig:error_taxonomy}) shows intra-batch substitution (IntraSub) scales monotonically with BCS across all three datasets: $\rho = 1.0$ ($p < 0.001$) for Few-NERD NER, $\rho = 0.954$ ($p < 0.001$) for FewRel sentence-level RE, and $\rho = 0.843$ ($p < 10^{-30}$, $n = 110$) for DocRED document-level RE. The slightly lower DocRED correlation reflects additional noise from document-level context and cross-sentence reasoning. In NER, missed rates are higher (43--65\%) and non-monotonic; confusable types impair entity detection itself. In DocRED, IntraSub accounts for 72\% of errors in high-BCS batches vs.\ 15\% in low-BCS batches; ExtraSub is zero (forced-choice format). Extreme-BCS batches produce $9.9\text{--}27.5\times$ more confusion than low-BCS batches.

These results establish two dimensions: \textbf{Dimension~1} (count predicts recall decline) and \textbf{Dimension~2} (confusability predicts type confusion). The full-range regression ($n = 69$, five count levels) serves as the primary separability test; the $2 \times 2$ factorial ($n = 54$) as a sensitivity check. We select the equivalence margin via convergent evidence: (i)~an interaction exceeding the primary BCS effect (IQR-predicted $\Delta = 2.2$\,pp; \S\ref{sec:practical_magnitude}) would represent ${\geq}100\%$ amplification, fundamentally altering whether the dimensions are independently addressable; (ii)~Cohen's $d = 0.5$ (medium effect) at the within-cell SD of 4.6\,pp yields $\pm 2.3$\,pp. Both converge on $\pm 2\text{--}3$\,pp. TOST \citep{lakens-2017-equivalence,schuirmann-1987-tost} on FewRel bounds the interaction at $\pm 3$\,pp ($p = 0.003$); at the stricter $\pm 2$\,pp margin, equivalence is marginal ($p = 0.047$) and inconclusive on the logit scale ($p = 0.209$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}). On DocRED ($n{=}55$ per count level), the raw factorial interaction is $-$9.4\,pp (95\% CI [$-$12.9, $-$6.0]), but this is confounded by narrower BCS ranges at count 32 (spread 0.26 vs.\ 0.38). Restricting both count levels to the overlapping BCS range [0.43, 0.70] reduces the interaction from 7.52\,pp (CI [1.49, 12.83]) to 0.93\,pp (CI [$-$7.13, 7.44])---consistent with bounded interaction, though the reduced sample ($n = 29{+}55$) has 80\% power only for effects ${\geq}10.5$\,pp (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}). In NER, batch-level BCS shows no predictive signal (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}), where NER errors distribute across miss, boundary, and type modes rather than concentrating in type substitution (relaxed matching confirms this null is not a metric artifact; Appendix~\ref{app:relaxed-matching}); the factorial test is inconclusive ($p = 0.50$).

\subsection{Centrality Confound on Homogeneous Schemas}
\label{sec:centrality_confound}

Per-type analysis on MAVEN reveals an anomalous \emph{positive} BCS--F1 correlation ($\rho = +0.184\text{--}0.208$, $p < 0.03$): FrameNet-derived types cluster tightly \citep{cai-isotropy-clusters-2021}, conflating confusability with centrality. Controlling for log frequency and degree centrality, the correlation vanishes ($p > 0.2$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_residualized_bcs}), though the pairwise SCS signal remains intact ($r = 0.129$, $p < 0.001$). Few-NERD's cross-domain schema shows the expected negative direction ($\rho = -0.293$, $p = 0.02$); mean BCS is reliable only for cross-domain schemas.

