\section{Factorial Analysis and Separability Test}
\label{sec:appendix_factorial}

To formally test whether count and confusability effects are additive, we fit a multiple regression (accuracy $\sim$ $\log_2$\,count $+$ BCS $+$ count$\times$BCS) across 69 batches from FewRel spanning five count levels ($k \in \{4, 8, 16, 32, 40\}$). Both main effects are significant (count: $p = 0.008$; BCS: $p = 0.0002$; $R^2 = 0.52$), but the interaction term is negligible (0.47\,pp). A TOST equivalence test with a $\pm 3$\,pp margin confirms that the interaction is bounded well below practical significance ($p_{\text{TOST}} = 0.003$); this supports bounded interaction between count and confusability effects.

A $2 \times 2$ factorial sensitivity check on FewRel (count $\in \{8, 16\}$, BCS low/high; $n = 54$) was inconclusive ($p_{\text{TOST}} = 0.56$) due to limited power; this does not contradict the primary finding but limits generalization beyond the regression framework. The Few-NERD factorial (\S\ref{sec:multi_channel}) similarly did not reach significance ($p = 0.50$). The regression approach provides the stronger test by leveraging the full count range.

\paragraph{Equivalence margin justification.}
We set the $\pm 3$\,pp margin based on three converging anchors. \emph{External}: evaluation variance in IE benchmarks typically spans 2--5\,pp across random seeds, prompt variations, and data splits \citep{card-2020-power,dror-2018-significance}; a within-margin interaction would be indistinguishable from routine evaluation noise. \emph{Internal}: $0.5 \times$ within-cell SD ($= 2.3$\,pp) corresponds to Cohen's $d \approx 0.5$ (medium effect threshold; \citealt{lakens-2017-equivalence}), cross-validated against the IQR-predicted confusability main effect of 2.2\,pp (\S\ref{sec:practical_magnitude})---an interaction exceeding 3\,pp would represent ${>}130\%$ amplification. \emph{Sensitivity}: Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity} shows equivalence holds from $\pm 2$\,pp ($p = 0.047$) through $\pm 5$\,pp ($p < 0.001$) on the probability scale; the conclusion is not margin-dependent.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{crrl}
\toprule
\textbf{Margin (pp)} & \textbf{$t_{\text{upper}}$} & \textbf{$p_{\text{TOST}}$} & \textbf{Conclusion} \\
\midrule
$\pm 1$ & $-0.59$ & $0.278$ & Inconclusive \\
$\pm 2$ & $-1.70$ & $0.047$ & Equivalent \\
$\pm 3$ & $-2.81$ & $0.003$ & Equivalent \\
$\pm 5$ & $-5.02$ & ${<}\,0.001$ & Equivalent \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{TOST sensitivity analysis across equivalence margins. The interaction effect (0.47\,pp, 90\% CI [$-$1.0, 2.0]\,pp) is bounded below 2\,pp at marginal significance ($p = 0.047$); the $\pm 3$\,pp choice does not drive the equivalence conclusion. At $\pm 1$\,pp, equivalence is inconclusive ($p = 0.278$), meaning interaction could reach approximately half the main effect magnitude.}
\label{tab:tost_sensitivity}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Logit-scale sensitivity.}
Because accuracy near 50\% compresses interaction effects on the linear scale, we refit the regression on logit-transformed accuracy. The logit-scale interaction (0.111 logit units, $p = 0.047$, 90\% CI [0.019, 0.203]) is marginally significant, and TOST at the equivalent $\pm 3$\,pp margin ($\pm 0.156$ logit at median accuracy) is inconclusive ($p_{\text{TOST}} = 0.209$). However, back-transforming the interaction to the probability scale at the observed floor ($p = 0.51$) yields 2.78\,pp, still within the $\pm 3$\,pp margin; at the median baseline ($p = 0.65$), the back-transformed effect is 2.53\,pp. The logit TOST is inconclusive due to limited power ($n = 69$, wide CI) rather than a large effect size: the point estimate remains below the equivalence bound at all observed baselines. We retain the linear-scale TOST as the primary test and report this logit analysis as a sensitivity check.

\paragraph{DocRED factorial (expanded).}
With expanded batches ($n{=}55$ per count level, up from $n{=}5$), the DocRED $2 \times 2$ factorial (count $\in \{16, 32\}$, BCS median split) yields an apparent interaction ($-$9.4\,pp, 95\% CI [$-$12.9, $-$6.0]): the BCS effect is $-$25.0\,pp at count 16 but $-$15.7\,pp at count 32. However, this attenuation is confounded by BCS range restriction: higher-count batches have narrower BCS ranges (spread 0.26 vs.\ 0.38), limiting achievable extremes.

\paragraph{Range-matched robustness check.}
To control for the BCS range confound, we restrict both count levels to the overlapping BCS range [0.43, 0.70]. This retains 29 of 55 batches at count 16 (all 55 at count 32 fall within the range). Within this matched range, the BCS effect at count 16 shrinks from 15.95\,pp to 9.37\,pp (reflecting the narrower BCS span), while count 32 remains at 8.44\,pp. The interaction drops from 7.52\,pp (95\% CI [1.49, 12.83]) to 0.93\,pp (95\% CI [$-$7.13, 7.44])---the CI spans zero, consistent with no interaction. Spearman correlations remain comparable ($\rho = -0.65$ at count 16, $\rho = -0.69$ at count 32), confirming that the underlying BCS--accuracy relationship is robust across counts while the raw interaction magnitude was an artifact of differing achievable BCS ranges. This result aligns with the FewRel TOST equivalence finding and supports bounded association as the cross-dataset pattern.

